a	2026-03-25-05-Teaching-AI-to-Verify-Sources2026-03-25-05-Ensinando-IA-a-Verificar-Fontes.pt
t	Teaching AI to Verify Its Sources
i	Alexandre Gomes Gaigalas – March 25, 2026
p	xLLMs confidently cite documentation that doesn't exist.
p	x“According to MDN, mPromise.allSettled()x returns a promise that resolves when all promises have completed.”
p	xThat sounds right. But MDN actually says “fulfills when all of the input's promises settle”. Different verb, different semantics. A human might not catch this. But a tool can.
p	xThis post shows how to make an AI verify its own claims before writing them, using kapysourced (https://github.com/alganet/apysource)x and a simple instruction file.
p	xA working demo of this workflow lives at kalganet/ecmascript-featurecardd (https://github.com/alganet/ecmascript-featurecard)x.
h	The Instruction File
p	xMost coding agents support project-level instruction files: a markdown file in the repo root that the agent reads before starting work. Drop this into yours:
c	C# Source Verification
c	
c	.When writing documentation that references external sources, prefer
c	.direct quotes over paraphrasing. Every factual claim about what a
c	.source says should be backed by an exact snippet from that source.
c	
c	.1. Before claiming S"X says Y"., verify the quote:
c	.       apysource locate S"<url>". S"<exact quote>"
c	.   If the snippet is not found, do not make the claim.
c	
c	.2. After verifying, register the claim:
c	.       apysource add sources.yaml S"<url>". S"<exact quote>". --label S"<claim name>"
c	
c	.3. After all documentation is written, run the full check:
c	.       apysource check sources.yaml
c	.   All checks must pass before considering the task complete.
p	xThink of it as an automated m[citation needed]x, the kind Wikipedia editors tag on unsourced claims, except here the agent is both the writer and the fact-checker, and the tag is enforced before the text is ever committed.
p	xThat's the entire integration. The agent reads this file, gains access to three shell commands, and follows the workflow: locate -> add -> check.
h	The Happy Path
p	xA user asks the agent to add mArray.findLast()x to the reference card.
p	xThe agent first verifies a quote from the MDN page:
c	.$. apysource locate \
c	.    S"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/findLast". \
c	.    S"iterates the array in reverse order"
c	C# Found in: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/.../Array/findLast
c	C# Targetter: section
c	
c	O-. AlabelO:. S''
c	.  AsectionO:. Array.prototype.findLast(), paragraph 2
c	.  AsnippetO:. iterates the array in reverse order
p	xThe snippet exists. The agent registers it:
c	.$. apysource add sources.yaml \
c	.    S"https://developer.mozilla.org/.../Array/findLast". \
c	.    S"iterates the array in reverse order". \
c	.    --label S"Array.findLast() iteration"
c	.  Located: section -> Array.prototype.findLast(), paragraph 2
c	.  Added new source: https://developer.mozilla.org/.../Array/findLast
c	.  Added fragment "Array.findLast() iteration" to sources.yaml
p	xThen runs the full check:
c	.$. apysource check sources.yaml
c	.  F[PASS]. Fragments: cache resolution............. 8/8
c	.  F[PASS]. Fragments: content extraction........... 8/8
c	.  F[PASS]. Fragments: snippet verified............. 8/8
p	xAll 8 fragments (the original 7 plus the new one) pass. The agent proceeds to regenerate the reference card with mpython generate.pyx.
h	The Hallucination
p	xNow the agent tries a different quote, one it thinks MDN says:
c	.$. apysource locate \
c	.    S"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/findLast". \
c	.    S"returns the first matching element from the end of the array"
c	EError: snippet not found in https://developer.mozilla.org/.../Array/findLast
p	xExit code 1. The instruction is clear: “If the snippet is not found, do not make the claim.”
p	xThe agent doesn't write the hallucinated quote. Instead, it goes back to mlocatex with a shorter phrase, finds what MDN actually says, and uses that instead.
p	xThis is not post-hoc correction. The agent never wrote the wrong claim. The verification happened before the documentation was produced.
h	CI as Safety Net
p	xEven with the instruction file, agents make mistakes. The CI pipeline is the final gate:
c	C# .github/workflows/verify-sources.yml
c	AnameO:. Verify Sources
c	AonO:. O[.push, pull_requestO]
c	AjobsO:
c	.  AcheckO:
c	.    Aruns-onO:. ubuntu-latest
c	.    AstepsO:
c	.      O-. AusesO:. actions/checkout@v4
c	.      O-. ArunO:. pip install apysource
c	.      O-. ArunO:. apysource check sources.yaml
p	xIf any quote drifts, whether the agent introduced it or MDN changed the page, the build fails. The msources.yamlx manifest is the contract between the documentation and its sources.
h	Final Remarks
p	xWithout source verification, AI-generated documentation is a liability. You can't distinguish accurate citations from hallucinations without manually checking every link.
p	xWith apysource in the loop, the agent becomes a verified documentation author. Every claim is grounded. Every quote is checked. The provenance graph records when it was verified and what the outcome was.
p	xThe msources.yamlx file is a machine-readable bibliography. It can generate reference cards, feed dashboards, or serve as an audit trail.
r	
